ComfyUI for Debian/Ubuntu (asahi-debian-packages)
=================================================

Upstream ships ComfyUI as a Python source tree you run inside a
virtualenv; there is no Linux binary release and no arm64 build of the
desktop app. This package stages the upstream tree, unmodified, in
/usr/lib/comfyui and adds /usr/bin/comfyui.

First run
---------
    comfyui

creates ~/.local/share/comfyui/venv, installs the CPU build of PyTorch
from https://download.pytorch.org/whl/cpu plus upstream's
requirements.txt (about 1 GB of downloads, 2.5 GB on disk, several
minutes), copies the
models/ folder skeleton, custom_nodes/ and input/ into
~/.local/share/comfyui, and starts the server on http://127.0.0.1:8188.
Later runs skip the install. After a package upgrade the launcher sees
the new version stamp and re-runs pip against the new requirements.txt.

Arguments are passed to ComfyUI's main.py:

    comfyui --auto-launch            open the browser when ready
    comfyui --listen 0.0.0.0         serve on the LAN
    comfyui --help                   everything else

Where things live
-----------------
Everything user-owned is under ~/.local/share/comfyui (override with
COMFYUI_HOME): models/, custom_nodes/, input/, output/, user/ (settings,
workflows, comfyui.db, comfyui.log), temp/ and venv/. Put checkpoints in
models/checkpoints/ and so on, exactly as in a git checkout.

extra_model_paths.yaml: upstream reads it from the source directory,
which is /usr/lib/comfyui here and root-owned. Use
    comfyui --extra-model-paths-config ~/.local/share/comfyui/extra_model_paths.yaml
instead.

Custom nodes that need extra Python packages: install them into the
venv, e.g.
    ~/.local/share/comfyui/venv/bin/pip install -r ~/.local/share/comfyui/custom_nodes/<node>/requirements.txt
ComfyUI-Manager can do this itself; it needs git (Recommends).

Performance on Asahi Linux
--------------------------
PyTorch has no GPU backend for Apple Silicon under Linux (no CUDA, no
MPS), so everything runs on the CPU. Expect minutes per SD1.5 image and
much longer for SDXL/Flux. Use small models and low step counts. If you
have a different accelerator, point COMFYUI_TORCH_INDEX at the matching
PyTorch wheel index before the first run (or delete venv/ and rerun);
the launcher then stops adding --cpu.

Removing
--------
apt remove comfyui removes /usr/lib/comfyui and the launcher only.
Your models, outputs and the venv stay in ~/.local/share/comfyui;
delete that directory yourself if you want them gone.
